  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nCold front continues to slip thru the forecast area. Rain\nshowers at this point are largely anafrontal, with a fairly\nwell defined wind shift boundary pushing southward on radar. So\nvery warm temps will get knocked back to the low 70s as that\npasses, and then rain cooled air will drop things a bit more\ninto the 60s thru the early evening hours. Overall not expecting\nmuch rain still, with amounts at or less than a tenth of an\ninch. \n\nOnce the front clears there will be some high clouds to contend\nwith early, but generally I anticipate that we will be able to\nradiate down into the 30s and 40s. Any location that saw some\nrain will be a decent candidate for radiation fog, and I have\nmaintained fog in the major river valleys. \n\nSat will be a seasonable day with little in the way of weather\nimpacts expected. Overnight however will be one of the cooler\nnights so far this season. Still at this point I do not\nanticipate 30s reaching too far into the active growing season\nzones and thus a need for frost headlines. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure exits the region on Sunday as an upper level \ntrough swings in from the Great Lakes Region. Clouds are going \nto build in early which is going to keep afternoon temperatures \nin the low to mid-60s. These clouds are also going to make for a\nmore mild night as temperatures only fall into the low to mid \n40s.\n\nA strong cold front crosses on Monday and it continues to look \nlike it is going to be mostly dry. The latest NBM probabilities \nof measurable precipitation remain less than 30% south of the \nmountains. The immediate coast has a slightly higher chance \n(30-40%) as guidance starts to show a weak coastal low \ngenerating at the base of the trough. Otherwise, any meaningful \nchances are confined to the International Boarder where rain \nshowers may briefly transition to light snow if they linger \nlonger enough into Monday night. The bigger impact from this \nfrontal passage is going to be the cold, dry air that fills in \nbehind it. 850 mb temperatures drop to -7 to 0 C by early \nTuesday so I would anticipate it being a cold night, but as the\nlow pressure waves phase over the Canadian Maritimes the \npressure gradient tightens and the resulting breezy winds should\nkeep the area from decoupling. North of the mountains \ntemperatures fall into the upper 20s and low 30s, and to the \nsouth they fall into the mid to upper 30s. The breezy winds \ncarry through the day Tuesday, so despite a sunny day it is \nlikely going to be one of the coldest days of the season so far.\nWith 850 mb temperatures as cold as they are high temperatures \ntop out in the upper 40s and low 50s in the north, and in the \nmid to upper 50s to the south. Depending on how the wind gusts \npan out it could feel a bit colder. Tuesday night looks like the\ncoldest night of the season so far and certainly will be if the\npressure gradient slackens enough for winds to go calm. The \nlatest NBM is hedging that way, bringing near freezing \ntemperatures all the way down the coast. Many areas south of the\nmountains may see their first hard freeze (at the very least \nfirst frost), but most of these zones also ended their window \nfor Frost/Freeze products yesterday. The coastal plain is the \nlast area that can still have them issued and if favorable \nradiational cooling pans out, I can see that being necessary.\n\nHigh pressure slides just south of the region Wednesday which \nshould keep us sunny and dry, but precipitation chances may \nreturn late in the week as long term guidance suggests another \ntrough with low pressure somewhere in the vicinity.",
